Produce JSON conforming to CVSelection: selected_project_ids, project_selection_reasons, cv_title, skills_profile_id, selected_skills, skills_order, warnings. Do not return experiences, experience IDs or rewritten bullets. Use experience evidence only as context for generated fields.

Project output rule:
- Decide selected_project_ids first: exactly 3 unique projects, with a minimum and maximum of 3.
- Choose the strongest direct matches first, then primary projects with the best documented transferable strengths to reach 3. Explain transferable relevance honestly without inventing direct domain experience.
- Admit at most one specialized project only for an explicit activation condition and distinct evidence. Never relax this requirement to reach 3 and never select disabled projects.
- Return project_selection_reasons with one entry per selected id: {"project_id": "...", "reason": "...", "offer_evidence": "exact excerpt from Main tasks, Required skills or Full job description", "complementary_value": "..."}.
- Include a nonempty complementary_value for a specialized project; explain why its evidence adds value beyond the other selected projects. Primary projects selected for transferable strengths also require an exact offer excerpt and an honest explanation of the connection.
- Do not use generic keyword overlap as a reason to activate a specialized project. Keep selection reasons internal; do not include them in the CV text.
- Do not invent projects or project claims; every selected id must come from the provided profile projects.
- If fewer than 3 projects can be selected under these rules, report the limitation in warnings instead of fabricating evidence or duplicating IDs. The application will reject an incomplete selection.
- When naming a project in text, use the display name after "::" exactly; never derive a display name from the internal project id.

Skills output rule:
- Fill selected_skills as a list of blocks: {"category_id": "...", "skills": ["..."]}.
- category_id must be one of the ids in allowed_skills_by_category.
- skills must be exact skill names from the matching allowed_skills_by_category block.
- Include every required or strongly requested offer skill that exists in allowed_skills.
- Use core_skills_by_category as a baseline only inside categories that are relevant to the offer.
- Use matching_keywords_for_profile_selection_not_display only for reasoning about the right skill profile/category; do not copy those keywords into selected_skills unless the exact term is also listed in allowed_skills_by_category.
- Do not cap selected_skills by number, but do not pad the section. Every displayed skill must add value for this specific application.

